oy who was seen getting into difficulty  yesterday evening his family have been  supported by specially trained officers  especially trained officers to assisting  the family of the man who is still the  subject of an ongoing search here at  arnsley near wakefield he was seen going  into the water at about 5 30 last night  getting into difficulty the emergency  services were called helicopters came  we've seen uh inshore rescue and search  units uh on the river uh on the uh  reservoir sorry uh in  in various uh in various boats and so on  and underwater search teams are on site  at the moment although i have to say  there doesn't seem to be much activity  on the reservoir  certainly for the last couple of hours  or so although the police do assure me  that the searches are continuing we've  seen a scene of crime forensic van  appear in the last hour or so whether or  not that means that  that something has been discovered we  are still not sure police haven't  confirmed that as yet but they say that  the searches are ongoing and they say  that this is another example of  highlighting the dangers of swimming in  open water any water that's below 15  degrees c  is deemed to be cold water according to  the emergency services and any water of  that temperature or below can cause the  body to go into cold water shock as it's  known once the body enters the water it  effectively creates a muscle spasm and a  breath spasm which if you're underwater  at the time can of course mean that the  lungs are almost instantly filled with  water and the muscles are in spasms so  it's very difficult to swim the rnli say  that their advice is float to survive if  you do find yourself in cold water you  do find yourself getting into difficulty  try and just remain as calm as possible  float on your back until your body gets  used to the water shock and then your  muscles will start to work again and you  can then begin to try and swim to safety  the advice is if you see anyone getting  